linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round;stroke-dasharray:2 3"/><line x1="161" y1="74" x2="161" y2="120" style="stroke:var(--fig-grid,#d1d5db);stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round;stroke-dasharray:2 3"/><polygon points="46,146 52.3,143 52.3,149" style="fill:var(--fig-muted,#6b7280);stroke:none"/><line x1="50.9" y1="146" x2="409.1" y2="146" style="stroke:var(--fig-muted,#6b7280);stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><polygon points="414,146 407.7,149 407.7,143" style="fill:var(--fig-muted,#6b7280);stroke:none"/><text x="230" y="158" font-size="12" text-anchor="middle" dominant-baseline="central" style="fill:currentColor">30d</text><line x1="46" y1="74" x2="46" y2="150" style="stroke:var(--fig-grid,#d1d5db);stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round;stroke-dasharray:2 3"/><line x1="414" y1="74" x2="414" y2="150" style="stroke:var(--fig-grid,#d1d5db);stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round;stroke-dasharray:2 3"/><text x="452" y="182" font-size="12" text-anchor="end" dominant-baseline="central" style="fill:var(--fig-muted,#6b7280)">not to scale</text></svg>', 'The Sun with the Earth at distance d, where the flux is 1.36 kW m⁻², and Neptune at 30d. Not to scale.'),
('32986d1c-166c-5a3c-9c4a-1128758296e2', '8695e833-9d29-4367-bfd6-0c20aebb2b1d', '26443cb9-a498-4768-a082-b6366197eb43', 'a-level', '31.2', '9702 coursebook 31.2 · Luminosity and radiant flux intensity', 29, 29, 'Star P has 4 times the luminosity of star Q and is twice as far away. How do their fluxes at the Earth compare?', NULL, 2, 'multiple_choice'::answer_type, 'They are equal', NULL, false, NULL, false, NULL, 'F ∝ L/d², and 4 ÷ 2² = 1, so the fluxes are equal. Mark scheme (3 marks). C1: F ∝ L/d². C1: ratio 1. A1: C (They are equal).', 3, '<svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 460 190" role="img" aria-labelledby="fg6d8e52fd-t fg6d8e52fd-d" style="font-family:ui-sans-serif,system-ui,-apple-system,''Segoe UI'',Roboto,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif;max-width:100%;height:auto"><title id="fg6d8e52fd-t">Stars P and Q</title><desc id="fg6d8e52fd-d">Star Q of luminosity L at distance d from the Earth, and star P of luminosity 4L at distance 2d.</desc><line x1="46" y1="62" x2="414" y2="62" style="stroke:var(--fig-grid,#d1d5db);stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round;stroke-dasharray:5 4"/><circle cx="46" cy="62" r="6" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:2.0;fill:var(--fig-fill,rgba(29,78,216,0.10));stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><text x="46" y="32" font-size="12" text-anchor="middle" dominant-baseline="central" style="fill:currentColor;font-weight:600">Earth</text><line x1="241" y1="62" x2="246" y2="62" style="stroke:var(--fig-accent-2,#b45309);stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="237.8" y1="69.8" x2="241.3" y2="73.3" style="stroke:var(--fig-accent-2,#b45309);stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="230" y1="73" x2="230" y2="78" style="stroke:var(--fig-accent-2,#b45309);stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="222.2" y1="69.8" x2="218.7" y2="73.3" style="stroke:var(--fig-accent-2,#b45309);stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="219" y1="62" x2="214" y2="62" style="stroke:var(--fig-accent-2,#b45309);stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="222.2" y1="54.2" x2="218.7" y2="50.7" style="stroke:var(--fig-accent-2,#b45309);stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="230" y1="51" x2="230" y2="46" style="stroke:var(--fig-accent-2,#b45309);stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="237.8" y1="54.2" x2="241.3" y2="50.7" style="stroke:var(--fig-accent-2,#b45309);stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><circle cx="230" cy="62" r="8" style="stroke:var(--fig-accent-2,#b45309);stroke-width:2.0;fill:var(--fig-fill,rgba(29,78,216,0.10));stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><text x="230" y="32" font-size="12" text-anchor="middle" dominant-baseline="central" style="fill:currentColor;font-weight:600">Q</text><text x="230" y="90" font-size="12" text-anchor="middle" dominant-baseline="central" style="fill:currentColor">L</text><line x1="425" y1="62" x2="430" y2="62" style="stroke:var(--fig-accent-2,#b45309);stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="421.8" y1="69.8" x2="425.3" y2="73.3" style="stroke:var(--fig-accent-2,#b45309);stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="414" y1="73" x2="414" y2="78" style="stroke:var(--fig-accent-2,#b45309);stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="406.2" y1="69.8" x2="402.7" y2="73.3" style="stroke:var(--fig-accent-2,#b45309);stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="403" y1="62" x2="398" y2="62" style="stroke:var(--fig-accent-2,#b45309);stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="406.2" y1="54.2" x2="402.7" y2="50.7" style="stroke:var(--fig-accent-2,#b45309);stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="414" y1="51" x2="414" y2="46" style="stroke:var(--fig-accent-2,#b45309);stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="421.8" y1="54.2" x2="425.3" y2="50.7" style="stroke:var(--fig-accent-2,#b45309);stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><circle cx="414" cy="62" r="8" style="stroke:var(--fig-accent-2,#b45309);stroke-width:2.0;fill:var(--fig-fill,rgba(29,78,216,0.10));stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><text x="414" y="32" font-size="12" text-anchor="middle" dominant-baseline="central" style="fill:currentColor;font-weight:600">P</text><text x="414" y="90" font-size="12" text-anchor="middle" dominant-baseline="central" style="fill:currentColor">4L</text><polygon points="46,116 52.3,113 52.3,119" style="fill:var(--fig-muted,#6b7280);stroke:none"/><line x1="50.9" y1="116" x2="225.1" y2="116" style="stroke:var(--fig-muted,#6b7280);stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><polygon points="230,116 223.7,119 223.7,113" style="fill:var(--fig-muted,#6b7280);stroke:none"/><text x="138" y="128" font-size="12" text-anchor="middle" dominant-baseline="central" style="fill:currentColor">d</text><line x1="46" y1="74" x2="46" y2="120" style="stroke:var(--fig-grid,#d1d5db);stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round;stroke-dasharray:2 3"/><line x1="230" y1="74" x2="230" y2="120" style="stroke:var(--fig-grid,#d1d5db);stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round;stroke-dasharray:2 3"/><polygon points="46,146 52.3,143 52.3,149" style="fill:var(--fig-muted,#6b7280);stroke:none"/><line x1="50.9" y1="146" x2="409.1" y2="146" style="stroke:var(--fig-muted,#6b7280);stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><polygon points="414,146 407.7,149 407.7,143" style="fill:var(--fig-muted,#6b7280);stroke:none"/><text x="230" y="158" font-size="12" text-anchor="middle" dominant-baseline="central" style="fill:currentColor">2d</text><line x1="46" y1="74" x2="46" y2="150" style="stroke:var(--fig-grid,#d1d5db);stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round;stroke-dasharray:2 3"/><line x1="414" y1="74" x2="414" y2="150" style="stroke:var(--fig-grid,#d1d5db);stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round;stroke-dasharray:2 3"/></svg>', 'Star Q of luminosity L at distance d from the Earth, and star P of luminosity 4L at distance 2d.'),
('f5cd5bc9-72d6-5268-99b3-d9c43674b31c', '8695e833-9d29-4367-bfd6-0c20aebb2b1d', '26443cb9-a498-4768-a082-b6366197eb43', 'a-level', '31.2', '9702 coursebook 31.2 · Luminosity and radiant flux intensity', 30, 30, 'A star is 3.1 × 10¹⁷ m away and its luminosity is 1.5 × 10²⁷ W. Calculate the power received by a telescope mirror of area 10 m².', NULL, 2, 'numeric'::answer_type, '1.242e-8', 'W', false, NULL, false, NULL, 'F = 1.5 × 10²⁷ ÷ (4π × (3.1 × 10¹⁷)²) = 1.242 × 10⁻⁹ W m⁻². P = 10 × 1.242 × 10⁻⁹ = 1.24 × 10⁻⁸ W. Mark scheme (3 marks). C1: flux. C1: P = FA. A1: P = 1.24 × 10⁻⁸ W.', 3, NULL, NULL),
('1df9c918-5032-5528-b6cc-dfd8d0b56594', '8695e833-9d29-4367-bfd6-0c20aebb2b1d', '26443cb9-a498-4768-a082-b6366197eb43', 'a-level', '31.2', '9702 coursebook 31.2 · Luminosity and radiant flux intensity', 31, 31, 'The flux from a star is measured as 4.5 × 10⁻¹⁰ W m⁻² and its distance is 12 light-years (1 ly = 9.46 × 10¹⁵ m). Calculate its luminosity in units of 10²⁶ W.', NULL, 2, 'numeric'::answer_type, '0.7287', '10²⁶ W', false, NULL, false, NULL, 'd = 12 × 9.46 × 10¹⁵ = 1.135 × 10¹⁷ m. L = 4π × (1.135 × 10¹⁷)² × 4.5 × 10⁻¹⁰ = 7.29 × 10²⁵ W. Mark scheme (3 marks). C1: ly to m. C1: L = 4πd²F. A1: L = 0.729 × 10²⁶ W.', 3, NULL, NULL),
('a746388e-e385-594d-86f0-509b63116d14', '8695e833-9d29-4367-bfd6-0c20aebb2b1d', '26443cb9-a498-4768-a082-b6366197eb43', 'a-level', '31.2', '9702 coursebook 31.2 · Luminosity and radiant flux intensity', 32, 32, 'What assumption is made when using F = L/4πd²?', NULL, 2, 'multiple_choice'::answer_type, 'The star radiates equally in all directions and no light is absorbed on the way', NULL, false, NULL, false, NULL, 'Isotropic emission and no absorption. Mark scheme (3 marks). C1: isotropic. C1: no absorption. A1: A (The star radiates equally in all directions and no light is absorbed on the way).', 3, NULL, NULL),
('52e8fc30-13bf-5fa7-8061-530d3e6906ba', '8695e833-9d29-4367-bfd6-0c20aebb2b1d', '26443cb9-a498-4768-a082-b6366197eb43', 'a-level', '31.2', '9702 coursebook 31.2 · Luminosity and radiant flux intensity', 33, 33, 'At what distance from the Sun (L = 3.8 × 10²⁶ W) is the flux 100 W m⁻²? Give your answer in units of 10¹¹ m.', NULL, 2, 'numeric'::answer_type, '5.499', '10¹¹ m', false, NULL, false, NULL, 'd = √(3.8 × 10²⁶ ÷ (4π × 100)) = 5.50 × 10¹¹ m. Mark scheme (3 marks). C1: rearranged. C1: value. A1: d = 5.50 × 10¹¹ m.', 3, NULL, NULL),
('34e8bacd-3cdc-58b3-b44d-0f3a9c6dc583', '8695e833-9d29-4367-bfd6-0c20aebb2b1d', '26443cb9-a498-4768-a082-b6366197eb43', 'a-level', '31.2', '9702 coursebook 31.2 · Luminosity and radiant flux intensity', 34, 34, 'The Sun''s luminosity is 3.8 × 10²⁶ W. A star has 25 times this luminosity and gives the same flux at the Earth as the Sun would give from 10 light-years away. Calculate the distance of the star in light-years (ly).', NULL, 2, 'numeric'::answer_type, '50', 'ly', false, NULL, false, NULL, 'Equal F: d ∝ √L, so d = 10 × √25 = 50 ly. Mark scheme (4 marks). M1: d ∝ √L for equal F. C1: √25. C1: value. A1: d = 50 ly.', 4, NULL, NULL),
('79b04b2f-af8d-5c62-af1d-cae8412efbb7', '8695e833-9d29-4367-bfd6-0c20aebb2b1d', '26443cb9-a498-4768-a082-b6366197eb43', 'a-level', '31.2', '9702 coursebook 31.2 · Luminosity and radiant flux intensity', 35, 35, 'A star''s flux at the Earth is halved. If its luminosity is unchanged, by what factor has its distance changed?', NULL, 2, 'multiple_choice'::answer_type, 'It has increased by a factor of √2', NULL, false, NULL, false, NULL, 'F ∝ 1/d², so d ∝ 1/√F. Mark scheme (3 marks). C1: d ∝ 1/√F. C1: √2. A1: D (It has increased by a factor of √2).', 3, NULL, NULL),
('1fb0b8c3-7cac-59dc-87ec-f7e513c1f5e8', '8695e833-9d29-4367-bfd6-0c20aebb2b1d', '26443cb9-a498-4768-a082-b6366197eb43', 'a-level', '31.2', '9702 coursebook 31.2 · Luminosity and radiant flux intensity', 36, 36, 'A probe measures the flux from the Sun as 4.0 kW m⁻². How far is it from the Sun (L = 3.8 × 10²⁶ W), in units of 10¹⁰ m?', NULL, 2, 'numeric'::answer_type, '8.695', '10¹⁰ m', false, NULL, false, NULL, 'd = √(3.8 × 10²⁶ ÷ (4π × 4.0 × 10³)) = 8.69 × 10¹⁰ m. Mark scheme (3 marks). C1: rearrange. C1: kW to W. A1: d = 8.69 × 10¹⁰ m.', 3, NULL, NULL),
('53fd42fc-4718-5930-bf38-7e1f304acea6', '8695e833-9d29-4367-bfd6-0c20aebb2b1d', '26443cb9-a498-4768-a082-b6366197eb43', 'a-level', '31.2', '9702 coursebook 31.2 · Luminosity and radiant flux intensity', 37, 37, 'Calculate (flux from the Sun at Venus, 1.08 × 10¹¹ m) ÷ (flux at the Earth, 1.50 × 10¹¹ m).', NULL, 2, 'numeric'::answer_type, '1.929', NULL, false, NULL, false, NULL, 'Ratio = (1.50 ÷ 1.08)² = 1.93. Mark scheme (3 marks). C1: inverse square. C1: value. A1: ratio = 1.93.', 3, '<svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 460 190" role="img" aria-labelledby="fg0a76d15f-t fg0a76d15f-d" style="font-family:ui-sans-serif,system-ui,-apple-system,''Segoe UI'',Roboto,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif;max-width:100%;height:auto"><title id="fg0a76d15f-t">Venus and the Earth</title><desc id="fg0a76d15f-d">The Sun with Venus 1.08 × 10¹¹ m away and the Earth 1.50 × 10¹¹ m away, drawn to scale.</desc><line x1="46" y1="62" x2="414" y2="62" style="stroke:var(--fig-grid,#d1d5db);stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round;stroke-dasharray:5 4"/><line x1="59" y1="62" x2="64" y2="62" style="stroke:var(--fig-accent-2,#b45309);stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="55.2" y1="71.2" x2="58.7" y2="74.7" style="stroke:var(--fig-accent-2,#b45309);stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="46" y1="75" x2="46" y2="80" style="stroke:var(--fig-accent-2,#b45309);stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="36.8" y1="71.2" x2="33.3" y2="74.7" style="stroke:var(--fig-accent-2,#b45309);stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="33" y1="62" x2="28" y2="62" style="stroke:var(--fig-accent-2,#b45309);stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="36.8" y1="52.8" x2="33.3" y2="49.3" style="stroke:var(--fig-accent-2,#b45309);stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="46" y1="49" x2="46" y2="44" style="stroke:var(--fig-accent-2,#b45309);stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="55.2" y1="52.8" x2="58.7" y2="49.3" style="stroke:var(--fig-accent-2,#b45309);stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><circle cx="46" cy="62" r="10" style="stroke:var(--fig-accent-2,#b45309);stroke-width:2.0;fill:var(--fig-fill,rgba(29,78,216,0.10));stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><text x="46" y="32" font-size="12" text-anchor="middle" dominant-baseline="central" style="fill:currentColor;font-weight:600">Sun</text><circle cx="311" cy="62" r="5" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:2.0;fill:var(--fig-fill,rgba(29,78,216,0.10));stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><text x="311" y="32" font-size="12" text-anchor="middle" dominant-baseline="central" style="fill:currentColor;font-weight:600">Venus</text><circle cx="414" cy="62" r="6" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:2.0;fill:var(--fig-fill,rgba(29,78,216,0.10));stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><text x="414" y="32" font-size="12" text-anchor="middle" dominant-baseline="central" style="fill:currentColor;font-weight:600">Earth</text><polygon points="46,116 52.3,113 52.3,119" style="fill:var(--fig-muted,#6b7280);stroke:none"/><line x1="50.9" y1="116" x2="306.1" y2="116" style="stroke:var(--fig-muted,#6b7280);stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><polygon points="311,116 304.6,119 304.6,113" style="fill:var(--fig-muted,#6b7280);stroke:none"/><text x="178.5" y="128" font-size="12" text-anchor="middle" dominant-baseline="central" style="fill:currentColor">1.08 × 10¹¹ m</text><line x1="46" y1="74" x2="46" y2="120" style="stroke:var(--fig-grid,#d1d5db);stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round;stroke-dasharray:2 3"/><line x1="311" y1="74" x2="311" y2="120" style="stroke:var(--fig-grid,#d1d5db);stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round;stroke-dasharray:2 3"/><polygon points="46,146 52.3,143 52.3,149" style="fill:var(--fig-muted,#6b7280);stroke:none"/><line x1="50.9" y1="146" x2="409.1" y2="146" style="stroke:var(--fig-muted,#6b7280);stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><polygon points="414,146 407.7,149 407.7,143" style="fill:var(--fig-muted,#6b7280);stroke:none"/><text x="230" y="158" font-size="12" text-anchor="middle" dominant-baseline="central" style="fill:currentColor">1.50 × 10¹¹ m</text><line x1="46" y1="74" x2="46" y2="150" style="stroke:var(--fig-grid,#d1d5db);stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round;stroke-dasharray:2 3"/><line x1="414" y1="74" x2="414" y2="150" style="stroke:var(--fig-grid,#d1d5db);stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round;stroke-dasharray:2 3"/></svg>', 'The Sun with Venus 1.08 × 10¹¹ m away and the Earth 1.50 × 10¹¹ m away, drawn to scale.'),
('4365f7a7-8f85-56fe-80ec-651f8024648c', '8695e833-9d29-4367-bfd6-0c20aebb2b1d', '26443cb9-a498-4768-a082-b6366197eb43', 'a-level', '31.2', '9702 coursebook 31.2 · Luminosity and radiant flux intensity', 38, 38, 'A galaxy contains 10¹¹ stars, each of about the Sun''s luminosity. How does the flux from the galaxy compare with that from a single such star at the same distance?', NULL, 2, 'multiple_choice'::answer_type, 'It is 10¹¹ times larger', NULL, false, NULL, false, NULL, 'Luminosities add; flux ∝ L. Mark scheme (4 marks). C1: luminosities add. C1: F ∝ L. C1: factor 10¹¹. A1: B (It is 10¹¹ times larger).', 4, NULL, NULL),
('54d2573a-b052-5c7b-96e5-b425da480898', '8695e833-9d29-4367-bfd6-0c20aebb2b1d', '26443cb9-a498-4768-a082-b6366197eb43', 'a-level', '31.2', '9702 coursebook 31.2 · Luminosity and radiant flux intensity', 39, 39, 'A star''s flux is 7.2 × 10⁻⁹ W m⁻² and its luminosity is 3.6 × 10²⁷ W. Calculate its distance in units of 10¹⁷ m.', NULL, 2, 'numeric'::answer_type, '1.995', '10¹⁷ m', false, NULL, false, NULL, 'd = √(3.6 × 10²⁷ ÷ (4π × 7.2 × 10⁻⁹)) = 1.99 × 10¹⁷ m. Mark scheme (3 marks). C1: rearrange. C1: value. A1: d = 1.99 × 10¹⁷ m.', 3, NULL, NULL),
('fd663a60-720d-5b6a-95e0-31b825a42cf2', '8695e833-9d29-4367-bfd6-0c20aebb2b1d', '26443cb9-a498-4768-a082-b6366197eb43', 'a-level', '31.2', '9702 coursebook 31.2 · Luminosity and radiant flux intensity', 40, 40, 'A star is 15 light-years away and gives the same flux at the Earth as a star of luminosity 2.0 × 10²⁶ W at 5.0 light-years. Calculate the first star''s luminosity in units of 10²⁷ W.', NULL, 2, 'numeric'::answer_type, '1.8', '10²⁷ W', false, NULL, false, NULL, 'Equal F: L ∝ d², so L = 2.0 × 10²⁶ × (15 ÷ 5.0)² = 1.8 × 10²⁷ W. Mark scheme (3 marks). C1: L ∝ d². C1: value. A1: L = 1.8 × 10²⁷ W.', 3, '<svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 460 190" role="img" aria-labelledby="fg22133c5d-t fg22133c5d-d" style="font-family:ui-sans-serif,system-ui,-apple-system,''Segoe UI'',Roboto,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif;max-width:100%;height:auto"><title id="fg22133c5d-t">Two stars giving equal flux</title><desc id="fg22133c5d-d">A star of luminosity 2.0 × 10²⁶ W 5.0 light-years from the Earth and a second star 15 light-years away that gives the same flux.</desc><line x1="46" y1="62" x2="414" y2="62" style="stroke:var(--fig-grid,#d1d5db);stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round;stroke-dasharray:5 4"/><circle cx="46" cy="62" r="6" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:2.0;fill:var(--fig-fill,rgba(29,78,216,0.10));stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><text x="46" y="32" font-size="12" text-anchor="middle" dominant-baseline="central" style="fill:currentColor;font-weight:600">Earth</text><line x1="179.7" y1="62" x2="184.7" y2="62" style="stroke:var(--fig-accent-2,#b45309);stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="176.4" y1="69.8" x2="180" y2="73.3" style="stroke:var(--fig-accent-2,#b45309);stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="168.7" y1="73" x2="168.7" y2="78" style="stroke:var(--fig-accent-2,#b45309);stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="160.9" y1="69.8" x2="157.4" y2="73.3" style="stroke:var(--fig-accent-2,#b45309);stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="157.7" y1="62" x2="152.7" y2="62" style="stroke:var(--fig-accent-2,#b45309);stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="160.9" y1="54.2" x2="157.4" y2="50.7" style="stroke:var(--fig-accent-2,#b45309);stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="168.7" y1="51" x2="168.7" y2="46" style="stroke:var(--fig-accent-2,#b45309);stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="176.4" y1="54.2" x2="180" y2="50.7" style="stroke:var(--fig-accent-2,#b45309);stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><circle cx="168.7" cy="62" r="8" style="stroke:var(--fig-accent-2,#b45309);stroke-width:2.0;fill:var(--fig-fill,rgba(29,78,216,0.10));stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><text x="168.7" y="32" font-size="12" text-anchor="middle" dominant-baseline="central" style="fill:currentColor;font-weight:600">second</text><text x="168.7" y="90" font-size="12" text-anchor="middle" dominant-baseline="central" style="fill:currentColor">2.0 × 10²⁶ W</text><line x1="425" y1="62" x2="430" y2="62" style="stroke:var(--fig-accent-2,#b45309);stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="421.8" y1="69.8" x2="425.3" y2="73.3" style="stroke:var(--fig-accent-2,#b45309);stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="414" y1="73" x2="414" y2="78" style="stroke:var(--fig-accent-2,#b45309);stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="406.2" y1="69.8" x2="402.7" y2="73.3" style="stroke:var(--fig-accent-2,#b45309);stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="403" y1="62" x2="398" y2="62" style="stroke:var(--fig-accent-2,#b45309);stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="406.2" y1="54.2" x2="402.7" y2="50.7" style="stroke:var(--fig-accent-2,#b45309);stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="414" y1="51" x2="414" y2="46" style="stroke:var(--fig-accent-2,#b45309);stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="421.8" y1="54.2" x2="425.3" y2="50.7" style="stroke:var(--fig-accent-2,#b45309);stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><circle cx="414" cy="62" r="8" style="stroke:var(--fig-accent-2,#b45309);stroke-width:2.0;fill:var(--fig-fill,rgba(29,78,216,0.10));stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><text x="414" y="32" font-size="12" text-anchor="middle" dominant-baseline="central" style="fill:currentColor;font-weight:600">first</text><text x="414" y="90" font-size="12" text-anchor="middle" dominant-baseline="central" style="fill:currentColor">L = ?</text><polygon points="46,116 52.3,113 52.3,119" style="fill:var(--fig-muted,#6b7280);stroke:none"/><line x1="50.9" y1="116" x2="163.8" y2="116" style="stroke:var(--fig-muted,#6b7280);stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><polygon points="168.7,116 162.3,119 162.3,113" style="fill:var(--fig-muted,#6b7280);stroke:none"/><text x="107.3" y="128" font-size="12" text-anchor="middle" dominant-baseline="central" style="fill:currentColor">5.0 light-years</text><line x1="46" y1="74" x2="46" y2="120" style="stroke:var(--fig-grid,#d1d5db);stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round;stroke-dasharray:2 3"/><line x1="168.7" y1="74" x2="168.7" y2="120" style="stroke:var(--fig-grid,#d1d5db);stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round;stroke-dasharray:2 3"/><polygon points="46,146 52.3,143 52.3,149" style="fill:var(--fig-muted,#6b7280);stroke:none"/><line x1="50.9" y1="146" x2="409.1" y2="146" style="stroke:var(--fig-muted,#6b7280);stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><polygon points="414,146 407.7,149 407.7,143" style="fill:var(--fig-muted,#6b7280);stroke:none"/><text x="230" y="158" font-size="12" text-anchor="middle" dominant-baseline="central" style="fill:currentColor">15 light-years</text><line x1="46" y1="74" x2="46" y2="150" style="stroke:var(--fig-grid,#d1d5db);stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round;stroke-dasharray:2 3"/><line x1="414" y1="74" x2="414" y2="150" style="stroke:var(--fig-grid,#d1d5db);stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round;stroke-dasharray:2 3"/></svg>', 'A star of luminosity 2.0 × 10²⁶ W 5.0 light-years from the Earth and a second star 15 light-years away that gives the same flux.'),
('bb80c252-aae2-5a66-8edb-970aeaf74d8c', '8695e833-9d29-4367-bfd6-0c20aebb2b1d', '26443cb9-a498-4768-a082-b6366197eb43', 'a-level', '31.2', '9702 coursebook 31.2 · Luminosity and radiant flux intensity', 41, 41, 'Proxima Centauri is 4.0 × 10¹⁶ m away and gives a flux of 3.6 × 10⁻¹¹ W m⁻². (a) Calculate its luminosity. (b) Calculate (its luminosity) ÷ (the Sun''s, 3.8 × 10²⁶ W). Give only the answer to (b), in units of 10⁻³.', NULL, 3, 'numeric'::answer_type, '0.001905', '10⁻³', false, NULL, false, NULL, '(a) L = 4π × (4.0 × 10¹⁶)² × 3.6 × 10⁻¹¹ = 7.238 × 10²³ W. (b) 7.238 × 10²³ ÷ 3.8 × 10²⁶ = 0.00190. Mark scheme (5 marks). C1: L = 4πd²F. C1: value. M1: ratio. C1: value. A1: ratio = 0.00190 10⁻³.', 5, NULL, NULL),
('03709aa3-b6fa-5724-9a65-4f4b656e06aa', '8695e833-9d29-4367-bfd6-0c20aebb2b1d', '26443cb9-a498-4768-a082-b6366197eb43', 'a-level', '31.2', '9702 coursebook 31.2 · Luminosity and radiant flux intensity', 42, 42, 'A binary system has two stars at the same distance. Together they give a flux of 6.0 × 10⁻¹⁰ W m⁻²; star 1 has twice the luminosity of star 2. The system is 3.0 × 10¹⁷ m away. (a) Calculate the total luminosity. (b) Calculate the luminosity of star 2. (c) Calculate the flux from star 1 alone. Give only the answer to (b), in units of 10²⁶ W.', NULL, 3, 'numeric'::answer_type, '2.262', '10²⁶ W', false, NULL, false, NULL, '(a) L = 4π × (3.0 × 10¹⁷)² × 6.0 × 10⁻¹⁰ = 6.786 × 10²⁶ W. (b) L₂ = 6.786 × 10²⁶ ÷ 3 = 2.26 × 10²⁶ W. (c) 2/3 × 6.0 × 10⁻¹⁰ = 4.0 × 10⁻¹⁰ W m⁻². Mark scheme (6 marks). C1: total L. M1: share 2 : 1. C1: value. C1: flux of star 1. B1: fluxes add. A1: L₂ = 2.26 × 10²⁶ W.', 6, NULL, NULL),
('bea53591-eb1f-52a1-9285-9fed20714889', '8695e833-9d29-4367-bfd6-0c20aebb2b1d', '26443cb9-a498-4768-a082-b6366197eb43', 'a-level', '31.2', '9702 coursebook 31.2 · Luminosity and radiant flux intensity', 43, 43, 'A space telescope can detect stars that give a flux of at least 1.0 × 10⁻¹⁸ W m⁻². (a) Calculate the greatest distance at which it can detect a star like the Sun (3.8 × 10²⁶ W). (b) Express it in light-years (1 ly = 9.46 × 10¹⁵ m). Give only the answer to (b), in units of 10⁵ ly.', NULL, 3, 'numeric'::answer_type, '5.813', '10⁵ ly', false, NULL, false, NULL, '(a) d = √(3.8 × 10²⁶ ÷ (4π × 1.0 × 10⁻¹⁸)) = 5.499 × 10²¹ m. (b) 5.499 × 10²¹ ÷ 9.46 × 10¹⁵ = 5.81 × 10⁵ ly. Mark scheme (5 marks). C1: rearrange. C1: value. M1: m to ly. C1: value. A1: d = 5.81 × 10⁵ ly.', 5, NULL, NULL),
('27578f11-8151-5b57-9695-d1129249c1c2', '8695e833-9d29-4367-bfd6-0c20aebb2b1d', '26443cb9-a498-4768-a082-b6366197eb43', 'a-level', '31.2', '9702 coursebook 31.2 · Luminosity and radiant flux intensity', 44, 44, 'An exoplanet orbits 1.2 × 10¹¹ m from a star of luminosity 5.0 × 10²⁶ W. (a) Calculate the flux at the planet. (b) The planet has radius 6.0 × 10⁶ m and absorbs 70% of the radiation falling on its cross-section. Calculate the power absorbed. Give only the answer to (b), in units of 10¹⁶ W.', NULL, 3, 'numeric'::answer_type, '21.88', '10¹⁶ W', false, NULL, false, NULL, '(a) F = 5.0 × 10²⁶ ÷ (4π × (1.2 × 10¹¹)²) = 2763 W m⁻². (b) P = 0.70 × 2763 × π × (6.0 × 10⁶)² = 2.19 × 10¹⁷ W. Mark scheme (6 marks). C1: flux. M1: cross-sectional area πr². C1: × 0.70. C1: substitution. C1: value. A1: P = 21.9 × 10¹⁶ W.', 6, NULL, NULL),
('e77efc7a-4de1-59a7-acef-863e2ef526bb', '8695e833-9d29-4367-bfd6-0c20aebb2b1d', '26443cb9-a498-4768-a082-b6366197eb43', 'a-level', '31.2', '9702 coursebook 31.2 · Luminosity and radiant flux intensity', 45, 45, 'Two stars have luminosities in the ratio 9 : 1 and give fluxes at the Earth in the ratio 1 : 4. Calculate (distance of the more luminous) ÷ (distance of the other).', NULL, 3, 'numeric'::answer_type, '6', NULL, false, NULL, false, NULL, 'd ∝ √(L/F): ratio = √(9 × 4) = 6.0. Mark scheme (5 marks). M1: d = √(L/4πF). C1: ratio of L. C1: ratio of F. C1: value. A1: ratio = 6.0.', 5, NULL, NULL),
('1c21aa5c-54c4-5827-94fa-a7d3414c774d', '8695e833-9d29-4367-bfd6-0c20aebb2b1d', '26443cb9-a498-4768-a082-b6366197eb43', 'a-level', '31.2', '9702 coursebook 31.2 · Luminosity and radiant flux intensity', 46, 46, 'The Sun''s luminosity is 3.8 × 10²⁶ W. (a) Calculate the solar flux at the Earth (1.50 × 10¹¹ m). (b) Calculate the total power intercepted by the Earth (radius 6.37 × 10⁶ m). (c) Calculate the fraction of the Sun''s output that the Earth intercepts. Give only the answer to (c), in units of 10⁻¹⁰.', NULL, 3, 'numeric'::answer_type, '4.509e-10', '10⁻¹⁰', false, NULL, false, NULL, '(a) F = 3.8 × 10²⁶ ÷ (4π × (1.50 × 10¹¹)²) = 1344 W m⁻². (b) P = 1344 × π × (6.37 × 10⁶)² = 1.713 × 10¹⁷ W. (c) πR² ÷ 4πd² = (6.37 × 10⁶)² ÷ (4 × (1.50 × 10¹¹)²) = 4.51 × 10⁻¹⁰. Mark scheme (6 marks). C1: flux. C1: cross-section. C1: power. M1: fraction. C1: value. A1: fraction = 4.51 × 10⁻¹⁰ 10⁻¹⁰.', 6, NULL, NULL),
('6f95f193-7fdc-54ca-b24b-eb5440eea337', '8695e833-9d29-4367-bfd6-0c20aebb2b1d', '26443cb9-a498-4768-a082-b6366197eb43', 'a-level', '31.2', '9702 coursebook 31.2 · Luminosity and radiant flux intensity', 47, 47, 'A Cepheid in the Andromeda galaxy has a luminosity of 1.2 × 10³⁰ W and gives a flux of 4.0 × 10⁻¹⁵ W m⁻². (a) Calculate the distance to Andromeda. (b) Express it in Mpc (1 Mpc = 3.09 × 10²² m).', NULL, 3, 'numeric'::answer_type, '0.1581', 'Mpc', false, NULL, false, NULL, '(a) d = √(1.2 × 10³⁰ ÷ (4π × 4.0 × 10⁻¹⁵)) = 4.886 × 10²¹ m. (b) 4.886 × 10²¹ ÷ 3.09 × 10²² = 0.158 Mpc. Mark scheme (5 marks). C1: rearrange. C1: value. M1: m to Mpc. C1: value. A1: d = 0.158 Mpc.', 5, NULL, NULL),
('a9045d87-6848-5900-ad78-81bdb2c582b3', '8695e833-9d29-4367-bfd6-0c20aebb2b1d', '26443cb9-a498-4768-a082-b6366197eb43', 'a-level', '31.2', '9702 coursebook 31.2 · Luminosity and radiant flux intensity', 48, 48, 'A star of luminosity L at distance d gives a flux F. (a) Show that a star of luminosity 4L at distance 3d gives a flux of 4F/9. (b) Calculate this flux when F = 9.0 × 10⁻⁹ W m⁻².', NULL, 3, 'numeric'::answer_type, '4e-9', 'W m⁻²', false, NULL, false, NULL, '(a) F′ = 4L ÷ (4π(3d)²) = (4/9) × L/(4πd²) = 4F/9. (b) F′ = 4 ÷ 9 × 9.0 × 10⁻⁹ = 4.0 × 10⁻⁹ W m⁻². Mark scheme (6 marks). M1: substitute 4L and 3d. B1: 4F/9 shown. C1: substitution. C1: value. B1: units. A1: F′ = 4.0 × 10⁻⁹ W m⁻².', 6, '<svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 460 190" role="img" aria-labelledby="fg960d9049-t fg960d9049-d" style="font-family:ui-sans-serif,system-ui,-apple-system,''Segoe UI'',Roboto,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif;max-width:100%;height:auto"><title id="fg960d9049-t">Two stars</title><desc id="fg960d9049-d">A star of luminosity L at distance d from the Earth giving flux F, and a star of luminosity 4L at distance 3d.</desc><line x1="46" y1="62" x2="414" y2="62" style="stroke:var(--fig-grid,#d1d5db);stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round;stroke-dasharray:5 4"/><circle cx="46" cy="62" r="6" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:2.0;fill:var(--fig-fill,rgba(29,78,216,0.10));stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><text x="46" y="32" font-size="12" text-anchor="middle" dominant-baseline="central" style="fill:currentColor;font-weight:600">Earth</text><line x1="179.7" y1="62" x2="184.7" y2="62" style="stroke:var(--fig-accent-2,#b45309);stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="176.4" y1="69.8" x2="180" y2="73.3" style="stroke:var(--fig-accent-2,#b45309);stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="168.7" y1="73" x2="168.7" y2="78" style="stroke:var(--fig-accent-2,#b45309);stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="160.9" y1="69.8" x2="157.4" y2="73.3" style="stroke:var(--fig-accent-2,#b45309);stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="157.7" y1="62" x2="152.7" y2="62" style="stroke:var(--fig-accent-2,#b45309);stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="160.9" y1="54.2" x2="157.4" y2="50.7" style="stroke:var(--fig-accent-2,#b45309);stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="168.7" y1="51" x2="168.7" y2="46" style="stroke:var(--fig-accent-2,#b45309);stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="176.4" y1="54.2" x2="180" y2="50.7" style="stroke:var(--fig-accent-2,#b45309);stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><circle cx="168.7" cy="62" r="8" style="stroke:var(--fig-accent-2,#b45309);stroke-width:2.0;fill:var(--fig-fill,rgba(29,78,216,0.10));stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><text x="168.7" y="90" font-size="12" text-anchor="middle" dominant-baseline="central" style="fill:currentColor">L</text><line x1="425" y1="62" x2="430" y2="62" style="stroke:var(--fig-accent-2,#b45309);stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="421.8" y1="69.8" x2="425.3" y2="73.3" style="stroke:var(--fig-accent-2,#b45309);stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="414" y1="73" x2="414" y2="78" style="stroke:var(--fig-accent-2,#b45309);stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="406.2" y1="69.8" x2="402.7" y2="73.3" style="stroke:var(--fig-accent-2,#b45309);stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="403" y1="62" x2="398" y2="62" style="stroke:var(--fig-accent-2,#b45309);stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="406.2" y1="54.2" x2="402.7" y2="50.7" style="stroke:var(--fig-accent-2,#b45309);stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="414" y1="51" x2="414" y2="46" style="stroke:var(--fig-accent-2,#b45309);stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="421.8" y1="54.2" x2="425.3" y2="50.7" style="stroke:var(--fig-accent-2,#b45309);stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><circle cx="414" cy="62" r="8" style="stroke:var(--fig-accent-2,#b45309);stroke-width:2.0;fill:var(--fig-fill,rgba(29,78,216,0.10));stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><text x="414" y="90" font-size="12" text-anchor="middle" dominant-baseline="central" style="fill:currentColor">4L</text><polygon points="46,116 52.3,113 52.3,119" style="fill:var(--fig-muted,#6b7280);stroke:none"/><line x1="50.9" y1="116" x2="163.8" y2="116" style="stroke:var(--fig-muted,#6b7280);stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><polygon points="168.7,116 162.3,119 162.3,113" style="fill:var(--fig-muted,#6b7280);stroke:none"/><text x="107.3" y="128" font-size="12" text-anchor="middle" dominant-baseline="central" style="fill:currentColor">d</text><line x1="46" y1="74" x2="46" y2="120" style="stroke:var(--fig-grid,#d1d5db);stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round;stroke-dasharray:2 3"/><line x1="168.7" y1="74" x2="168.7" y2="120" style="stroke:var(--fig-grid,#d1d5db);stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round;stroke-dasharray:2 3"/><polygon points="46,146 52.3,143 52.3,149" style="fill:var(--fig-muted,#6b7280);stroke:none"/><line x1="50.9" y1="146" x2="409.1" y2="146" style="stroke:var(--fig-muted,#6b7280);stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><polygon points="414,146 407.7,149 407.7,143" style="fill:var(--fig-muted,#6b7280);stroke:none"/><text x="230" y="158" font-size="12" text-anchor="middle" dominant-baseline="central" style="fill:currentColor">3d</text><line x1="46" y1="74" x2="46" y2="150" style="stroke:var(--fig-grid,#d1d5db);stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round;stroke-dasharray:2 3"/><line x1="414" y1="74" x2="414" y2="150" style="stroke:var(--fig-grid,#d1d5db);stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round;stroke-dasharray:2 3"/></svg>', 'A star of luminosity L at distance d from the Earth giving flux F, and a star of luminosity 4L at distance 3d.'),
('a25073d1-6c8d-5625-8c20-8eb1ce592dd0', '8695e833-9d29-4367-bfd6-0c20aebb2b1d', '26443cb9-a498-4768-a082-b6366197eb43', 'a-level', '31.2', '9702 coursebook 31.2 · Luminosity and radiant flux intensity', 49, 49, 'A star 1.5 × 10¹⁸ m away gives a flux of 2.4 × 10⁻¹⁰ W m⁻². (a) Calculate its luminosity. (b) Calculate the flux it would give at 10 pc (1 pc = 3.09 × 10¹⁶ m). Give only the answer to (b), in units of 10⁻⁷ W m⁻².', NULL, 3, 'numeric'::answer_type, '0.05656', '10⁻⁷ W m⁻²', false, NULL, false, NULL, '(a) L = 4π × (1.5 × 10¹⁸)² × 2.4 × 10⁻¹⁰ = 6.786 × 10²⁷ W. (b) F = 2.4 × 10⁻¹⁰ × (1.5 × 10¹⁸ ÷ 3.09 × 10¹⁷)² = 5.66 × 10⁻⁹ W m⁻². Mark scheme (5 marks). C1: L. C1: 10 pc in m. M1: inverse square. C1: value. A1: F = 0.0566 × 10⁻⁷ W m⁻².', 5, NULL, NULL),
('fe41c519-cd43-5b07-b5b1-627ad303efce', '8695e833-9d29-4367-bfd6-0c20aebb2b1d', '26443cb9-a498-4768-a082-b6366197eb43', 'a-level', '31.2', '9702 coursebook 31.2 · Luminosity and radiant flux intensity', 50, 50, 'An astronomer measures fluxes from two Cepheids of the same period (so equal luminosity): 8.0 × 10⁻¹² W m⁻² from one in our galaxy at a known 6.0 × 10¹⁹ m, and 2.0 × 10⁻¹⁷ W m⁻² from one in another galaxy. (a) Explain why equal periods imply equal luminosities. (b) Calculate the distance to the other galaxy. Give only the answer to (b), in units of 10²² m.', NULL, 3, 'numeric'::answer_type, '3.795', '10²² m', false, NULL, false, NULL, '(a) The period–luminosity law links them. (b) d₂ = d₁√(F₁/F₂) = 6.0 × 10¹⁹ × √(8.0 × 10⁻¹² ÷ 2.0 × 10⁻¹⁷) = 3.79 × 10²² m. Mark scheme (6 marks). B1: period–luminosity. M1: d ∝ 1/√F. C1: flux ratio. C1: substitution. C1: value. A1: d = 3.79 × 10²² m.', 6, NULL, NULL)
