('4f27811c-8a8b-5dc4-a2cb-d00c72e8465c', '8695e833-9d29-4367-bfd6-0c20aebb2b1d', '2df83c3e-834f-517a-a55c-cf1ff80eccfb', 'a-level', '31.3', '9702 coursebook 31.3 · Stellar radii: Wien and Stefan–Boltzmann', 10, 10, 'How does the peak wavelength of a star''s spectrum change as its surface temperature rises?', NULL, 1, 'multiple_choice'::answer_type, 'It becomes shorter', NULL, false, NULL, false, NULL, 'Wien: λmax ∝ 1/T, so hotter stars peak at shorter wavelengths. Mark scheme (1 mark). B1: C (It becomes shorter).', 1, NULL, NULL),
('d758f3bb-4ffd-505e-926a-7569157c38a1', '8695e833-9d29-4367-bfd6-0c20aebb2b1d', '2df83c3e-834f-517a-a55c-cf1ff80eccfb', 'a-level', '31.3', '9702 coursebook 31.3 · Stellar radii: Wien and Stefan–Boltzmann', 11, 11, 'Rigel has a surface temperature of 12 000 K. Calculate the wavelength at which it emits most strongly, in nm. Take the Wien constant = 2.90 × 10⁻³ m K.', NULL, 1, 'numeric'::answer_type, '241.7 nm', 'nm', false, NULL, false, NULL, 'λ = 2.90 × 10⁻³ ÷ 12 000 = 2.417 × 10⁻⁷ m. In nm: 2.417 × 10⁻⁷ × 10⁹ = 242. Mark scheme (2 marks). C1: λmax = b/T. A1: λmax = 242 nm.', 2, NULL, NULL),
('b26e6eff-458f-50de-9e4e-859d69934636', '8695e833-9d29-4367-bfd6-0c20aebb2b1d', '2df83c3e-834f-517a-a55c-cf1ff80eccfb', 'a-level', '31.3', '9702 coursebook 31.3 · Stellar radii: Wien and Stefan–Boltzmann', 12, 12, 'Which equation is the Stefan–Boltzmann law for a star of radius r and surface temperature T?', NULL, 1, 'multiple_choice'::answer_type, 'L = 4πr²σT⁴', NULL, false, NULL, false, NULL, 'Power per unit area σT⁴ times the surface area 4πr². Mark scheme (1 mark). B1: A (L = 4πr²σT⁴).', 1, NULL, NULL),
('36072ad4-b90c-52ce-ad95-537498f0fbd5', '8695e833-9d29-4367-bfd6-0c20aebb2b1d', '2df83c3e-834f-517a-a55c-cf1ff80eccfb', 'a-level', '31.3', '9702 coursebook 31.3 · Stellar radii: Wien and Stefan–Boltzmann', 13, 13, 'A star has a radius of 5.0 × 10⁸ m and a surface temperature of 4000 K. Calculate its luminosity, in units of 10²⁵ W. Take σ = 5.67 × 10⁻⁸ W m⁻² K⁻⁴.', NULL, 1, 'numeric'::answer_type, '4.56', '10²⁵ W', false, NULL, false, NULL, 'L = 4πr²σT⁴ = 4π × (5.0 × 10⁸)² × 5.67 × 10⁻⁸ × 4000⁴ = 4.56 × 10²⁵ W. Mark scheme (2 marks). C1: L = 4πr²σT⁴. A1: L = 4.56 × 10²⁵ W.', 2, NULL, NULL),
('33fe5d17-46b4-595c-bf81-97cdf82b2410', '8695e833-9d29-4367-bfd6-0c20aebb2b1d', '2df83c3e-834f-517a-a55c-cf1ff80eccfb', 'a-level', '31.3', '9702 coursebook 31.3 · Stellar radii: Wien and Stefan–Boltzmann', 14, 14, 'What colour is a star with a very high surface temperature?', NULL, 1, 'multiple_choice'::answer_type, 'Blue-white', NULL, false, NULL, false, NULL, 'Its spectrum peaks at short (blue/ultraviolet) wavelengths. Mark scheme (1 mark). B1: D (Blue-white).', 1, NULL, NULL),
('b31db852-2040-54a1-8630-f5d41daa30ae', '8695e833-9d29-4367-bfd6-0c20aebb2b1d', '2df83c3e-834f-517a-a55c-cf1ff80eccfb', 'a-level', '31.3', '9702 coursebook 31.3 · Stellar radii: Wien and Stefan–Boltzmann', 15, 15, 'The surface temperature of a star is doubled while its radius stays the same. By what factor does its luminosity increase?', NULL, 1, 'numeric'::answer_type, '16', NULL, false, NULL, false, NULL, 'L ∝ T⁴: factor = 2⁴ = 16. Mark scheme (2 marks). C1: T⁴ dependence. A1: factor = 16.', 2, NULL, NULL),
('91e360d1-0ef0-5fb7-aae0-ab14c9db1cc5', '8695e833-9d29-4367-bfd6-0c20aebb2b1d', '2df83c3e-834f-517a-a55c-cf1ff80eccfb', 'a-level', '31.3', '9702 coursebook 31.3 · Stellar radii: Wien and Stefan–Boltzmann', 16, 16, 'What is meant by a black body?', NULL, 1, 'multiple_choice'::answer_type, 'An object that absorbs all radiation falling on it and emits the maximum possible at each wavelength for its temperature', NULL, false, NULL, false, NULL, 'Stars are close to black-body emitters. Mark scheme (1 mark). B1: B (An object that absorbs all radiation falling on it and emits the maximum possible at each wavelength for its temperature).', 1, NULL, NULL),
('b1644dc1-18a4-57ce-82fa-578c7547cca0', '8695e833-9d29-4367-bfd6-0c20aebb2b1d', '2df83c3e-834f-517a-a55c-cf1ff80eccfb', 'a-level', '31.3', '9702 coursebook 31.3 · Stellar radii: Wien and Stefan–Boltzmann', 17, 17, 'The radius of a star is tripled while its surface temperature stays the same. By what factor does its luminosity increase?', NULL, 1, 'numeric'::answer_type, '9', NULL, false, NULL, false, NULL, 'L ∝ r²: factor = 3² = 9. Mark scheme (2 marks). C1: r² dependence. A1: factor = 9.', 2, NULL, NULL),
('aa0243f6-32d0-5a50-a334-6c2cabc84236', '8695e833-9d29-4367-bfd6-0c20aebb2b1d', '2df83c3e-834f-517a-a55c-cf1ff80eccfb', 'a-level', '31.3', '9702 coursebook 31.3 · Stellar radii: Wien and Stefan–Boltzmann', 18, 18, 'What does σ represent in the Stefan–Boltzmann law?', NULL, 1, 'multiple_choice'::answer_type, 'The Stefan–Boltzmann constant, 5.67 × 10⁻⁸ W m⁻² K⁻⁴', NULL, false, NULL, false, NULL, 'It links the power per unit area to T⁴. Mark scheme (1 mark). B1: C (The Stefan–Boltzmann constant, 5.67 × 10⁻⁸ W m⁻² K⁻⁴).', 1, NULL, NULL),
('088b2379-0ed6-556c-b4c9-8c927a2648d1', '8695e833-9d29-4367-bfd6-0c20aebb2b1d', '2df83c3e-834f-517a-a55c-cf1ff80eccfb', 'a-level', '31.3', '9702 coursebook 31.3 · Stellar radii: Wien and Stefan–Boltzmann', 19, 19, 'A red dwarf emits most strongly at 1000 nm. Calculate its surface temperature. Take the Wien constant = 2.90 × 10⁻³ m K.', NULL, 1, 'numeric'::answer_type, '2900', 'K', false, NULL, false, NULL, 'T = 2.90 × 10⁻³ ÷ 1000 × 10⁻⁹ = 2900 K. Mark scheme (2 marks). C1: T = b/λmax. A1: T = 2900 K.', 2, '<svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 420 290" role="img" aria-labelledby="fg04577778-t fg04577778-d" style="font-family:ui-sans-serif,system-ui,-apple-system,''Segoe UI'',Roboto,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif;max-width:100%;height:auto"><title id="fg04577778-t">Spectrum of a red dwarf</title><desc id="fg04577778-d">Black-body spectrum of a red dwarf: relative intensity against wavelength from 0 to 3000 nm, peaking at 1000 nm, with the visible band shaded.</desc><line x1="24" y1="34" x2="24" y2="242" style="stroke:var(--fig-grid,#d1d5db);stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="150" y1="34" x2="150" y2="242" style="stroke:var(--fig-grid,#d1d5db);stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="276" y1="34" x2="276" y2="242" style="stroke:var(--fig-grid,#d1d5db);stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="402" y1="34" x2="402" y2="242" style="stroke:var(--fig-grid,#d1d5db);stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="24" y1="242" x2="402" y2="242" style="stroke:var(--fig-grid,#d1d5db);stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="24" y1="151.6" x2="402" y2="151.6" style="stroke:var(--fig-grid,#d1d5db);stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="24" y1="61.1" x2="402" y2="61.1" style="stroke:var(--fig-grid,#d1d5db);stroke-width:0.9;fil