('8f8644ac-0c01-55a9-8914-557d7b54b8ac', '8695e833-9d29-4367-bfd6-0c20aebb2b1d', '2df83c3e-834f-517a-a55c-cf1ff80eccfb', 'a-level', '31.3', '9702 coursebook 31.3 · Stellar radii: Wien and Stefan–Boltzmann', 20, 20, 'Which quantities must be known to find a star''s radius from the Stefan–Boltzmann law?', NULL, 1, 'multiple_choice'::answer_type, 'Its luminosity and its surface temperature', NULL, false, NULL, false, NULL, 'From L = 4πr²σT⁴, r = √(L/4πσT⁴), so both L and T are needed. Mark scheme (1 mark). B1: A (Its luminosity and its surface temperature).', 1, NULL, NULL),
('12e6c094-9671-5c60-897e-fa0304e392ee', '8695e833-9d29-4367-bfd6-0c20aebb2b1d', '2df83c3e-834f-517a-a55c-cf1ff80eccfb', 'a-level', '31.3', '9702 coursebook 31.3 · Stellar radii: Wien and Stefan–Boltzmann', 21, 21, 'Calculate the power radiated by each square metre of a star''s surface at 6000 K, in units of 10⁷ W m⁻². Take σ = 5.67 × 10⁻⁸ W m⁻² K⁻⁴.', NULL, 1, 'numeric'::answer_type, '7.348', '10⁷ W m⁻²', false, NULL, false, NULL, 'P/A = σT⁴ = 5.67 × 10⁻⁸ × 6000⁴ = 7.35 × 10⁷ W m⁻². Mark scheme (2 marks). C1: σT⁴. A1: P/A = 7.35 × 10⁷ W m⁻².', 2, NULL, NULL),
('1b477fee-1fe6-56fa-b671-17ac0ea36878', '8695e833-9d29-4367-bfd6-0c20aebb2b1d', '2df83c3e-834f-517a-a55c-cf1ff80eccfb', 'a-level', '31.3', '9702 coursebook 31.3 · Stellar radii: Wien and Stefan–Boltzmann', 22, 22, 'In which unit is the Wien constant measured?', NULL, 1, 'multiple_choice'::answer_type, 'm K', NULL, false, NULL, false, NULL, 'λmax T = constant, so the unit is metre kelvin. Mark scheme (1 mark). B1: D (m K).', 1, NULL, NULL),
('7a57c101-dfe9-5c4e-9bfc-115fd2e80453', '8695e833-9d29-4367-bfd6-0c20aebb2b1d', '2df83c3e-834f-517a-a55c-cf1ff80eccfb', 'a-level', '31.3', '9702 coursebook 31.3 · Stellar radii: Wien and Stefan–Boltzmann', 23, 23, 'Calculate (peak wavelength of a 3000 K star) ÷ (peak wavelength of a 9000 K star).', NULL, 1, 'numeric'::answer_type, '3', NULL, false, NULL, false, NULL, 'λmax ∝ 1/T: ratio = 9000 ÷ 3000 = 3.0. Mark scheme (2 marks). C1: inverse proportion. A1: ratio = 3.0.', 2, NULL, NULL),
('c09ab7d6-42ab-5155-beeb-35acd1a8e97b', '8695e833-9d29-4367-bfd6-0c20aebb2b1d', '2df83c3e-834f-517a-a55c-cf1ff80eccfb', 'a-level', '31.3', '9702 coursebook 31.3 · Stellar radii: Wien and Stefan–Boltzmann', 24, 24, 'Two stars have the same surface temperature, but one is much larger. Which statement is correct?', NULL, 1, 'multiple_choice'::answer_type, 'The larger star has the greater luminosity', NULL, false, NULL, false, NULL, 'L ∝ r² at fixed T; the peak wavelength depends only on T. Mark scheme (1 mark). B1: B (The larger star has the greater luminosity).', 1, NULL, NULL),
('09b1c802-c2cd-5db8-a094-3ab7cf24e6dd', '8695e833-9d29-4367-bfd6-0c20aebb2b1d', '2df83c3e-834f-517a-a55c-cf1ff80eccfb', 'a-level', '31.3', '9702 coursebook 31.3 · Stellar radii: Wien and Stefan–Boltzmann', 25, 25, 'A star''s spectrum peaks at 290 nm. Calculate its surface temperature. Take the Wien constant = 2.90 × 10⁻³ m K.', NULL, 1, 'numeric'::answer_type, '10000', 'K', false, NULL, false, NULL, 'T = 2.90 × 10⁻³ ÷ 290 × 10⁻⁹ = 1.00 × 10⁴ K. Mark scheme (1 mark). A1: T = 1.00 × 10⁴ K.', 1, '<svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 420 290" role="img" aria-labelledby="fg76743bcd-t fg76743bcd-d" style="font-family:ui-sans-serif,system-ui,-apple-system,''Segoe UI'',Roboto,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif;max-width:100%;height:auto"><title id="fg76743bcd-t">Spectrum of a hot star</title><desc id="fg76743bcd-d">Black-body spectrum of a star: relative intensity against wavelength from 0 to 1500 nm, peaking at 290 nm in the ultraviolet, with the visible band shaded.</desc><line x1="24" y1="34" x2="24" y2="242" style="stroke:var(--fig-grid,#d1d5db);stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="99.6" y1="34" x2="99.6" y2="242" style="stroke:var(--fig-grid,#d1d5db);stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="175.2" y1="34" x2="175.2" y2="242" style="stroke:var(--fig-grid,#d1d5db);stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="250.8" y1="34" x2="250.8" y2="242" style="stroke:var(--fig-grid,#d1d5db);stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="326.4" y1="34" x2="326.4" y2="242" style="stroke:var(--fig-grid,#d1d5db);stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="402" y1="34" x2="402" y2="242" style="stroke:var(--fig-grid,#d1d5db);stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="24" y1="242" x2="402" y2="242" style="stroke:var(--fig-grid,#d1d5db);stroke-width: