('1bf522a3-0b08-537b-8659-bcd6c2c4cde0', '8695e833-9d29-4367-bfd6-0c20aebb2b1d', '2df83c3e-834f-517a-a55c-cf1ff80eccfb', 'a-level', '31.3', '9702 coursebook 31.3 · Stellar radii: Wien and Stefan–Boltzmann', 28, 28, 'A red giant and a white dwarf have similar luminosities. What can be concluded?', NULL, 2, 'multiple_choice'::answer_type, 'The red giant is much larger and much cooler', NULL, false, NULL, false, NULL, 'L = 4πr²σT⁴: lower T must be offset by larger r. Mark scheme (3 marks). C1: L same. C1: cooler means larger. A1: C (The red giant is much larger and much cooler).', 3, NULL, NULL),
('d3871590-16a5-50fc-abcd-b1ae2949dcb1', '8695e833-9d29-4367-bfd6-0c20aebb2b1d', '2df83c3e-834f-517a-a55c-cf1ff80eccfb', 'a-level', '31.3', '9702 coursebook 31.3 · Stellar radii: Wien and Stefan–Boltzmann', 29, 29, 'Star A has twice the radius of star B and half its surface temperature. Calculate (luminosity of A) ÷ (luminosity of B).', NULL, 2, 'numeric'::answer_type, '0.25', NULL, false, NULL, false, NULL, 'L ∝ r²T⁴: ratio = 2² × (0.5)⁴ = 0.25. Mark scheme (3 marks). C1: r² factor. C1: T⁴ factor. A1: ratio = 0.250.', 3, NULL, NULL),
('f778328d-943e-5c01-ac70-37af5d638475', '8695e833-9d29-4367-bfd6-0c20aebb2b1d', '2df83c3e-834f-517a-a55c-cf1ff80eccfb', 'a-level', '31.3', '9702 coursebook 31.3 · Stellar radii: Wien and Stefan–Boltzmann', 30, 30, 'Treat the Sun as a black body whose surface is at 5800 K. Find the peak of its emission spectrum, giving the wavelength in nm. Take the Wien constant = 2.90 × 10⁻³ m K.', NULL, 2, 'numeric'::answer_type, '500 nm', 'nm', false, NULL, false, NULL, 'λ = 2.90 × 10⁻³ ÷ 5800 = 5.000 × 10⁻⁷ m. In nm: 5.000 × 10⁻⁷ × 10⁹ = 500. Mark scheme (3 marks). C1: Wien. C1: nm. A1: λmax = 500 nm.', 3, NULL, NULL),
('004048b7-334f-5382-b3e3-72a0791cd7b0', '8695e833-9d29-4367-bfd6-0c20aebb2b1d', '2df83c3e-834f-517a-a55c-cf1ff80eccfb', 'a-level', '31.3', '9702 coursebook 31.3 · Stellar radii: Wien and Stefan–Boltzmann', 31, 31, 'How does the shape of a black-body spectrum change as the temperature rises?', NULL, 2, 'multiple_choice'::answer_type, 'The peak moves to shorter wavelengths and the whole curve rises', NULL, false, NULL, false, NULL, 'Wien''s law and the Stefan–Boltzmann law together. Mark scheme (3 marks). C1: peak shifts shorter. C1: total power increases. A1: A (The peak moves to shorter wavelengths and the whole curve rises).', 3, NULL, NULL),
('019162d5-d17b-57f6-96f8-f70f3da9cf83', '8695e833-9d29-4367-bfd6-0c20aebb2b1d', '2df83c3e-834f-517a-a55c-cf1ff80eccfb', 'a-level', '31.3', '9702 coursebook 31.3 · Stellar radii: Wien and Stefan–Boltzmann', 32, 32, 'Calculate the total power output of a white dwarf 7.0 × 10⁶ m in radius whose surface is at 25 000 K. Give the answer in units of 10²⁵ W. Take σ = 5.67 × 10⁻⁸ W m⁻² K⁻⁴.', NULL, 2, 'numeric'::answer_type, '1.364', '10²⁵ W', false, NULL, false, NULL, 'L = 4π × (7.0 × 10⁶)² × 5.67 × 10⁻⁸ × 25 000⁴ = 1.36 × 10²⁵ W. Mark scheme (3 marks). C1: formula. C1: substitution. A1: L = 1.36 × 10²⁵ W.', 3, NULL, NULL),
('6a2003bb-985c-5a07-be79-b799bdf9216c', '8695e833-9d29-4367-bfd6-0c20aebb2b1d', '2df83c3e-834f-517a-a55c-cf1ff80eccfb', 'a-level', '31.3', '9702 coursebook 31.3 · Stellar radii: Wien and Stefan–Boltzmann', 33, 33, 'A star''s luminosity is 81 times that of the Sun and its surface temperature is the same as the Sun''s. Calculate (radius of the star) ÷ (radius of the Sun).', NULL, 2, 'numeric'::answer_type, '9', NULL, false, NULL, false, NULL, 'Same T: L ∝ r², so r ratio = √81 = 9.0. Mark scheme (4 marks). M1: L ∝ r² at fixed T. C1: √81. C1: value. A1: ratio = 9.0.', 4, NULL, NULL),
('ec57f1f6-74c8-5663-b896-6aff2ba2439c', '8695e833-9d29-4367-bfd6-0c20aebb2b1d', '2df83c3e-834f-517a-a55c-cf1ff80eccfb', 'a-level', '31.3', '9702 coursebook 31.3 · Stellar radii: Wien and Stefan–Boltzmann', 34, 34, 'Why can the radius of a distant star be found even though it cannot be seen as a disc?', NULL, 2, 'multiple_choice'::answer_type, 'Its luminosity (from flux and distance) and its temperature (from Wien''s law) give r through L = 4πr²σT⁴', NULL, false, NULL, false, NULL, 'Combine the two laws. Mark scheme (3 marks). C1: L known. C1: T from spectrum. A1: D (Its luminosity (from flux and distance) and its temperature (from Wien''s law) give r through L = 4πr²σT⁴).', 3, NULL, NULL),
('ba3c5d67-ea62-5a79-a36a-0d7df788bb34', '8695e833-9d29-4367-bfd6-0c20aebb2b1d', '2df83c3e-834f-517a-a55c-cf1ff80eccfb', 'a-level', '31.3', '9702 coursebook 31.3 · Stellar radii: Wien and Stefan–Boltzmann', 35, 35, 'A star has a radius of 2.5 × 10¹⁰ m and a luminosity of 4.0 × 10³⁰ W. Calculate its surface temperature. Take σ = 5.67 × 10⁻⁸ W m⁻² K⁻⁴.', NULL, 2, 'numeric'::answer_type, '9735', 'K', false, NULL, false, NULL, 'T = (L/4πr²σ)^(1/4) = (4.0 × 10³⁰ ÷ (4π × (2.5 × 10¹⁰)² × 5.67 × 10⁻⁸))^0.25 = 9740 K. Mark scheme (3 marks). C1: rearranged. C1: fourth root. A1: T = 9740 K.', 3, NULL, NULL),
('55079c7e-3401-5e1e-a2a4-9f3852439fc2', '8695e833-9d29-4367-bfd6-0c20aebb2b1d', '2df83c3e-834f-517a-a55c-cf1ff80eccfb', 'a-level', '31.3', '9702 coursebook 31.3 · Stellar radii: Wien and Stefan–Boltzmann', 36, 36, 'Calculate (power per unit area from a 10 000 K star) ÷ (power per unit area from a 5000 K star).', NULL, 2, 'numeric'::answer_type, '16', NULL, false, NULL, false, NULL, 'σT⁴ ratio = (10 000 ÷ 5000)⁴ = 16. Mark scheme (3 marks). C1: ∝ T⁴. C1: value. A1: ratio = 16.', 3, NULL, NULL),
('be034bf7-c36b-51b4-a499-ba4efc3d73c7', '8695e833-9d29-4367-bfd6-0c20aebb2b1d', '2df83c3e-834f-517a-a55c-cf1ff80eccfb', 'a-level', '31.3', '9702 coursebook 31.3 · Stellar radii: Wien and Stefan–Boltzmann', 37, 37, 'The Sun''s spectrum peaks in the visible region, at about 500 nm. Where does the spectrum of a star at 3000 K peak?', NULL, 2, 'multiple_choice'::answer_type, 'In the infrared', NULL, false, NULL, false, NULL, 'λmax = 2.90 × 10⁻³ ÷ 3000 ≈ 970 nm. Mark scheme (4 marks). C1: Wien. C1: ~970 nm. C1: infrared. A1: B (In the infrared).', 4, '<svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 420 290" role="img" aria-labelledby="fg9ebb6fc8-t fg9ebb6fc8-d" style="font-family:ui-sans-serif,system-ui,-apple-system,''Segoe UI'',Roboto,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif;max-width:100%;height:auto"><title id="fg9ebb6fc8-t">Spectrum of the Sun</title><desc id="fg9ebb6fc8-d">Black-body spectrum of the Sun: relative intensity against wavelength from 0 to 2000 nm, peaking at about 500 nm inside the shaded visible band (400–700 nm).</desc><line x1="24" y1="34" x2="24" y2="242" style="stroke:var(--fig-grid,#d1d5db);stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="118.5" y1="34" x2="118.5" y2="242" style="stroke:var(--fig-grid,#d1d5db);stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="213" y1="34" x2="213" y2="242" style="stroke:var(--fig-grid,#d1d5db);stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="307.5" y1="34" x2="307.5" y2="242" style="stroke:var(--fig-grid,#d1d5db);stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="402" y1="34" x2="402" y2="242" style="stroke:var(--fig-grid,#d1d5db);stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="24" y1="242" x2="402" y2="242" style="stroke:var(--fig-grid,#d1d5db);stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="24" y1="151.6" x2="402" y2="151.6" 