('0045a6fc-2fdb-5fa4-84bf-220212bbdf1a', '8695e833-9d29-4367-bfd6-0c20aebb2b1d', '2df83c3e-834f-517a-a55c-cf1ff80eccfb', 'a-level', '31.3', '9702 coursebook 31.3 · Stellar radii: Wien and Stefan–Boltzmann', 38, 38, 'Calculate the peak wavelength, in µm, for the cosmic microwave background at 2.7 K. Take the Wien constant = 2.90 × 10⁻³ m K.', NULL, 2, 'numeric'::answer_type, '1074 um', 'µm', false, NULL, false, NULL, 'λ = 2.90 × 10⁻³ ÷ 2.7 = 0.001074 m. In µm: 0.001074 × 10⁶ = 1070. Mark scheme (3 marks). C1: Wien. C1: m to µm. A1: λmax = 1070 µm.', 3, NULL, NULL),
('2e55f285-e517-5629-a3d6-f7552067cc6c', '8695e833-9d29-4367-bfd6-0c20aebb2b1d', '2df83c3e-834f-517a-a55c-cf1ff80eccfb', 'a-level', '31.3', '9702 coursebook 31.3 · Stellar radii: Wien and Stefan–Boltzmann', 39, 39, 'A star''s temperature rises by 10%. By what percentage does its luminosity rise, if its radius is unchanged?', NULL, 2, 'numeric'::answer_type, '46.41', '%', false, NULL, false, NULL, 'L ∝ T⁴: factor = 1.10⁴ = 1.464; rise = (1.464 − 1) × 100 = 46.4 %. Mark scheme (3 marks). C1: T⁴. C1: percentage. A1: rise = 46.4%.', 3, NULL, NULL),
('06c0316b-40c2-533d-a5f7-e084489faa89', '8695e833-9d29-4367-bfd6-0c20aebb2b1d', '2df83c3e-834f-517a-a55c-cf1ff80eccfb', 'a-level', '31.3', '9702 coursebook 31.3 · Stellar radii: Wien and Stefan–Boltzmann', 40, 40, 'Two stars have equal radii. One has a surface temperature of 7500 K and the other 5000 K. Calculate (luminosity of the hotter star) ÷ (luminosity of the cooler star).', NULL, 2, 'numeric'::answer_type, '5.062', NULL, false, NULL, false, NULL, 'Equal r: L ∝ T⁴, so ratio = (7500 ÷ 5000)⁴ = 5.06. Mark scheme (3 marks). C1: L ∝ T⁴ at fixed r. C1: value. A1: ratio = 5.06.', 3, NULL, NULL),
('a87656c5-1cc4-5642-86b3-5e94c726d246', '8695e833-9d29-4367-bfd6-0c20aebb2b1d', '2df83c3e-834f-517a-a55c-cf1ff80eccfb', 'a-level', '31.3', '9702 coursebook 31.3 · Stellar radii: Wien and Stefan–Boltzmann', 41, 41, 'A star has a luminosity of 3.8 × 10²⁶ W and a radius of 7.0 × 10⁸ m. Calculate the power emitted by each square metre of its surface, in units of 10⁷ W m⁻².', NULL, 2, 'numeric'::answer_type, '6.171', '10⁷ W m⁻²', false, NULL, false, NULL, 'P/A = L ÷ 4πr² = 3.8 × 10²⁶ ÷ (4π × (7.0 × 10⁸)²) = 6.17 × 10⁷ W m⁻². Mark scheme (3 marks). C1: area 4πr². C1: value. A1: P/A = 6.17 × 10⁷ W m⁻².', 3, NULL, NULL),
('dda81669-964c-5043-9e47-fcd3a8f83d59', '8695e833-9d29-4367-bfd6-0c20aebb2b1d', '2df83c3e-834f-517a-a55c-cf1ff80eccfb', 'a-level', '31.3', '9702 coursebook 31.3 · Stellar radii: Wien and Stefan–Boltzmann', 42, 42, 'A star''s spectrum peaks at 380 nm. Calculate the power radiated by each square metre of its surface, in units of 10⁸ W m⁻². Take the Wien constant = 2.90 × 10⁻³ m K and σ = 5.67 × 10⁻⁸ W m⁻² K⁻⁴.', NULL, 2, 'numeric'::answer_type, '1.923', '10⁸ W m⁻²', false, NULL, false, NULL, 'T = 2.90 × 10⁻³ ÷ 380 × 10⁻⁹ = 7632 K. P/A = σT⁴ = 5.67 × 10⁻⁸ × 7632⁴ = 1.92 × 10⁸ W m⁻². Mark scheme (3 marks). C1: T from Wien. C1: σT⁴. A1: P/A = 1.92 × 10⁸ W m⁻².', 3, '<svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 420 290" role="img" aria-labelledby="fg1a6184f6-t fg1a6184f6-d" style="font-family:ui-sans-serif,system-ui,-apple-system,''Segoe UI'',Roboto,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif;max-width:100%;height:auto"><title id="fg1a6184f6-t">Stellar spectrum</title><desc id="fg1a6184f6-d">Black-body spectrum of a star: relative intensity against wavelength from 0 to 1500 nm, peaking at 380 nm.</desc><line x1="24" y1="34" x2="24" y2="242" style="stroke:var(--fig-grid,#d1d5db);stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="99.6" y1="34" x2="99.6" y2="242" style="stroke:var(--fig-grid,#d1d5db);stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="175.2" y1="34" x2="175.2" y2="242" style="stroke:var(--fig-grid,#d1d5db);stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="250.8" y1="34" x2="250.8" y2="242" style="stroke:var(--fig-grid,#d1d5db);stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="326.4" y1="34" x2="326.4" y2="242" style="stroke:var(--fig-grid,#d1d5db);stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="402" y1="34" x2="402" y2="242" style="stroke:var(--fig-grid,#d1d5db);stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;