('1cdf1087-73f8-5016-8cc2-7d5161a8890b', '8695e833-9d29-4367-bfd6-0c20aebb2b1d', '2df83c3e-834f-517a-a55c-cf1ff80eccfb', 'a-level', '31.3', '9702 coursebook 31.3 · Stellar radii: Wien and Stefan–Boltzmann', 44, 44, 'A star 2.0 × 10¹⁷ m away gives a flux of 6.0 × 10⁻⁹ W m⁻², and its spectrum peaks at 600 nm. (a) Calculate its luminosity. (b) Calculate its surface temperature. (c) Calculate its radius. Give only the answer to (c), in units of 10⁸ m. Take the Wien constant = 2.90 × 10⁻³ m K and σ = 5.67 × 10⁻⁸ W m⁻² K⁻⁴.', NULL, 3, 'numeric'::answer_type, '27.85', '10⁸ m', false, NULL, false, NULL, '(a) L = 4π × (2.0 × 10¹⁷)² × 6.0 × 10⁻⁹ = 3.016 × 10²⁷ W. (b) T = 2.90 × 10⁻³ ÷ 600 × 10⁻⁹ = 4833 K. (c) r = √(3.016 × 10²⁷ ÷ (4π × 5.67 × 10⁻⁸ × (4833)⁴)) = 2.78 × 10⁹ m. Mark scheme (6 marks). C1: L = 4πd²F. C1: T from Wien. M1: r from Stefan–Boltzmann. C1: substitution. C1: value. A1: r = 27.8 × 10⁸ m.', 6, '<svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 420 290" role="img" aria-labelledby="fga3340d10-t fga3340d10-d" style="font-family:ui-sans-serif,system-ui,-apple-system,''Segoe UI'',Roboto,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif;max-width:100%;height:auto"><title id="fga3340d10-t">Stellar spectrum</title><desc id="fga3340d10-d">Black-body spectrum of a star: relative intensity against wavelength from 0 to 2500 nm, peaking at 600 nm.</desc><line x1="24" y1="34" x2="24" y2="242" style="stroke:var(--fig-grid,#d1d5db);stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="99.6" y1="34" x2="99.6" y2="242" style="stroke:var(--fig-grid,#d1d5db);stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="175.2" y1="34" x2="175.2" y2="242" style="stroke:var(--fig-grid,#d1d5db);stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="250.8" y1="34" x2="250.8" y2="242" style="stroke:var(--fig-grid,#d1d5db);stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="326.4" y1="34" x2="326.4" 