('6b0f9176-ffbc-50a2-88f6-0f2248903381', '8695e833-9d29-4367-bfd6-0c20aebb2b1d', '2df83c3e-834f-517a-a55c-cf1ff80eccfb', 'a-level', '31.3', '9702 coursebook 31.3 · Stellar radii: Wien and Stefan–Boltzmann', 45, 45, 'A red giant has a surface temperature of 3500 K and a luminosity 1000 times the Sun''s (Sun: 5800 K). (a) Write L ∝ r²T⁴ for both stars. (b) Calculate (radius of the giant) ÷ (radius of the Sun).', NULL, 3, 'numeric'::answer_type, '86.84', NULL, false, NULL, false, NULL, '(a) L_g/L_s = (r_g/r_s)² (T_g/T_s)⁴. (b) r_g/r_s = √(1000 × (5800 ÷ 3500)⁴) = 86.8. Mark scheme (5 marks). B1: ratio form. M1: rearrange for r ratio. C1: substitution. C1: value. A1: ratio = 86.8.', 5, NULL, NULL),
('00c34313-16ff-5b4a-ba69-ddb4e3ea62c1', '8695e833-9d29-4367-bfd6-0c20aebb2b1d', '2df83c3e-834f-517a-a55c-cf1ff80eccfb', 'a-level', '31.3', '9702 coursebook 31.3 · Stellar radii: Wien and Stefan–Boltzmann', 46, 46, 'The Sun (radius 6.96 × 10⁸ m, surface temperature 5800 K) radiates as a black body. (a) Calculate its luminosity. (b) Calculate the flux at the Earth, 1.50 × 10¹¹ m away. Give only the answer to (b), in kW m⁻². Take σ = 5.67 × 10⁻⁸ W m⁻² K⁻⁴.', NULL, 3, 'numeric'::answer_type, '1.381', 'kW m⁻²', false, NULL, false, NULL, '(a) L = 4π × (6.96 × 10⁸)² × 5.67 × 10⁻⁸ × 5800⁴ = 3.906 × 10²⁶ W. (b) F = 3.906 × 10²⁶ ÷ (4π × (1.50 × 10¹¹)²) = 1381 W m⁻². In kW m⁻²: 1381 ÷ 10³ = 1.38. Mark scheme (6 marks). C1: L. C1: substitution. M1: F = L/4πd². C1: value. C1: kW conversion. A1: F = 1.38 kW m⁻².', 6, NULL, NULL),
('585dad6b-852e-5173-9d3c-4b23f4a615db', '8695e833-9d29-4367-bfd6-0c20aebb2b1d', '2df83c3e-834f-517a-a55c-cf1ff80eccfb', 'a-level', '31.3', '9702 coursebook 31.3 · Stellar radii: Wien and Stefan–Boltzmann', 47, 47, 'Two stars have the same luminosity. Star X has a peak wavelength of 400 nm and star Y one of 800 nm. (a) Calculate (temperature of X) ÷ (temperature of Y). (b) Calculate (radius of Y) ÷ (radius of X).', NULL, 3, 'numeric'::answer_type, '4', NULL, false, NULL, false, NULL, '(a) T ∝ 1/λmax: 800 ÷ 400 = 2.0. (b) Equal L: r ∝ 1/T², so r_Y/r_X = (2.0)² = 4.0. Mark scheme (5 marks). C1: temperature ratio. M1: r ∝ 1/T² for equal L. C1: substitution. C1: value. A1: ratio = 4.0.', 5, '<svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 420 290" role="img" aria-labelledby="fge3bde4bf-t fge3bde4bf-d" style="font-family:ui-sans-serif,system-ui,-apple-system,''Segoe UI'',Roboto,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif;max-width:100%;height:auto"><title id="fge3bde4bf-t">Two stars of equal luminosity</title><desc id="fge3bde4bf-d">Spectra of stars X and Y, which have the same luminosity (equal areas under the curves): X peaks at 400 nm and Y, with the lower and broader curve, at 800 nm.</desc><line x1="24" y1="34" x2="24" y2="242" style="stroke:var(--fig-grid,#d1d5db);stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="99.6" y1="34" x2="99.6" y2="242" style="stroke:var(--fig-grid,#d1d5db);stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="175.2" y1="34" x2="175.2" y2="242" style="stroke:var(--fig-grid,#d1d5db);stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="250.8" y1="34" x2="250.8" y2="242" style="stroke:var(--fig-grid,#d1d5db);stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="326.4" y1="34" x2="326.4" y2="242" style="stroke:var(--fig-grid,#d1d5db);stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="402" y1="34" x2="402" y2="