">X</text><line x1="350.1" y1="14" x2="372.1" y2="14" style="stroke:var(--fig-accent-2,#b45309);stroke-width:2.0;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round;stroke-dasharray:6 4"/><text x="378.1" y="14" font-size="12" text-anchor="start" dominant-baseline="central" style="fill:currentColor">Y</text></svg>', 'Spectra of stars X and Y, which have the same luminosity (equal areas under the curves): X peaks at 400 nm and Y, with the lower and broader curve, at 800 nm.'),
('534352f7-d992-5120-8743-b62afd55c577', '8695e833-9d29-4367-bfd6-0c20aebb2b1d', '2df83c3e-834f-517a-a55c-cf1ff80eccfb', 'a-level', '31.3', '9702 coursebook 31.3 · Stellar radii: Wien and Stefan–Boltzmann', 48, 48, 'A white dwarf of radius 6.0 × 10⁶ m has a luminosity of 2.0 × 10²⁴ W. (a) Calculate its surface temperature. (b) Calculate its peak wavelength in nm. Give only the answer to (b). Take σ = 5.67 × 10⁻⁸ W m⁻² K⁻⁴ and the Wien constant = 2.90 × 10⁻³ m K.', NULL, 3, 'numeric'::answer_type, '173.5 nm', 'nm', false, NULL, false, NULL, '(a) T = (2.0 × 10²⁴ ÷ (4π × (6.0 × 10⁶)² × 5.67 × 10⁻⁸))^0.25 = 1.671 × 10⁴ K. (b) λ = 2.90 × 10⁻³ ÷ 1.671 × 10⁴ = 1.735 × 10⁻⁷ m. In nm: 1.735 × 10⁻⁷ × 10⁹ = 174. Mark scheme (6 marks). C1: T rearranged. C1: value. M1: Wien. C1: conversion. C1: value. A1: λmax = 174 nm.', 6, NULL, NULL),
('3e3e45d6-7b3f-5e3a-bb9a-a063cb1d0cdc', '8695e833-9d29-4367-bfd6-0c20aebb2b1d', '2df83c3e-834f-517a-a55c-cf1ff80eccfb', 'a-level', '31.3', '9702 coursebook 31.3 · Stellar radii: Wien and Stefan–Boltzmann', 49, 49, 'Star P has radius 3.0 × 10⁹ m and temperature 8000 K; star Q has radius 1.5 × 10¹⁰ m and temperature 4000 K. (a) Calculate both luminosities. (b) Calculate (luminosity of Q) ÷ (luminosity of P). Take σ = 5.67 × 10⁻⁸ W m⁻² K⁻⁴.', NULL, 3, 'numeric'::answer_type, '1.562', NULL, false, NULL, false, NULL, '(a) P: 2.627 × 10²⁸ W; Q: 4.104 × 10²⁸ W. (b) Ratio = (1.5 × 10¹⁰ ÷ 3.0 × 10⁹)² × (4000 ÷ 8000)⁴ = 1.56. Mark scheme (5 marks). C1: L of P. C1: L of Q. M1: ratio of r²T⁴. C1: value. A1: ratio = 1.56.', 5, NULL, NULL),
('08ac2754-c20f-53c1-b34e-32dd6143b6aa', '8695e833-9d29-4367-bfd6-0c20aebb2b1d', '2df83c3e-834f-517a-a55c-cf1ff80eccfb', 'a-level', '31.3', '9702 coursebook 31.3 · Stellar radii: Wien and Stefan–Boltzmann', 50, 50, 'A star of luminosity 3.0 × 10²⁸ W at 4.0 × 10¹⁸ m has a spectrum peaking at 500 nm. (a) Calculate the flux at the Earth. (b) Calculate its temperature. (c) Calculate its radius. Give only the answer to (c), in units of 10⁹ m. Take the Wien constant = 2.90 × 10⁻³ m K and σ = 5.67 × 10⁻⁸ W m⁻² K⁻⁴.', NULL, 3, 'numeric'::answer_type, '6.1', '10⁹ m', false, NULL, false, NULL, '(a) F = 3.0 × 10²⁸ ÷ (4π × (4.0 × 10¹⁸)²) = 1.49 × 10⁻¹⁰ W m⁻². (b) T = 2.90 × 10⁻³ ÷ 500 × 10⁻⁹ = 5800 K. (c) r = √(3.0 × 10²⁸ ÷ (4π × 5.67 × 10⁻⁸ × 5800⁴)) = 6.10 × 10⁹ m. Mark scheme (6 marks). C1: flux. C1: T. M1: r from L and T. C1: substitution. C1: value. A1: r = 6.10 × 10⁹ m.', 6, NULL, NULL),
('58627b7b-8ebb-5e3b-89cc-7509476f9bbe', '8695e833-9d29-4367-bfd6-0c20aebb2b1d', '2df83c3e-834f-517a-a55c-cf1ff80eccfb', 'a-level', '31.3', '9702 coursebook 31.3 · Stellar radii: Wien and Stefan–Boltzmann', 51, 51, 'The cosmic microwave background behaves as a black body at 2.73 K. (a) Calculate its peak wavelength in mm. (b) Calculate the power per unit area it radiates, in units of 10⁻⁶ W m⁻². Take the Wien constant = 2.90 × 10⁻³ m K and σ = 5.67 × 10⁻⁸ W m⁻² K⁻⁴.', NULL, 3, 'numeric'::answer_type, '3.149', '10⁻⁶ W m⁻²', false, NULL, false, NULL, '(a) λ = 2.90 × 10⁻³ ÷ 2.73 = 0.001062 m, about 1.06 mm. (b) σT⁴ = 5.67 × 10⁻⁸ × 2.73⁴ = 3.15 × 10⁻⁶ W m⁻². Mark scheme (5 marks). C1: Wien. C1: mm. M1: σT⁴. C1: value. A1: P/A = 3.15 × 10⁻⁶ W m⁻².', 5, NULL, NULL)
INSERT INTO problem_options (id, problem_id, option_text, option_letter, is_correct, "order") VALUES
('09a7d8bb-8591-5a7c-84ac-4aee27067071', '4f27811c-8a8b-5dc4-a2cb-d00c72e8465c', 'It becomes longer', 'A', false, 0),
('d068f6d5-e17c-5c6d-bbc1-bc4f10ac0e0b', '4f27811c-8a8b-5dc4-a2cb-d00c72e8465c', 'It stays the same', 'B', false, 1),
